I want to play a game of Wordle. I have a secret 5-letter word from the PostgreSQL SQL dialect and you have to guess it.

How it works:
You give me a 5-letter guess in lower case ASCII characters only.
I enter your guess into the game and report back the result as a table with these columns:
  • Guess — the guess number
  • Word — the word you guessed
  • Distance — an integer showing how far the guess is from the secret word (lower is better, 0 means you got it)
  • Tip — a text breakdown in the form:
      "soundex diff: X, metaphone diff: Y, levenshtein diff: Z"
      Each value is a non-negative integer; smaller means closer along that dimension.
      Use all three together to reason about how phonetically and orthographically close your guess is to the secret word.

Your constraint: Every response must start with your Next Guess in bold, followed by a brief explanation of what the tip values tell you and how they are guiding your next choice.
When the distance reaches 0, you won! You may then share an interesting fact about the secret PostgreSQL keyword.

Do you understand? If so, give me your first 5-letter opening guess now.
